<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>What is a function?</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00817</md:content-id>
    <md:title>What is a function?</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5e79fadf-d2c6-4584-b3f2-816e176f2a2a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (15 minutes)</title>

<para>This activity will get students interacting directly with a function so they can discover the basic principles of a function. It is important that students can name the parts of a function, come up with an initial definition of a function, be able to describe a function, and see that a function always behaves consistently. </para>

<title>Launch</title>

<list type="enumerated">
  <item>Allow students to work independently to answer questions on their activity sheet, stopping where indicated to have small-group or whole-class discussions.</item>
  <item>Circulate as students work on Part 2 of the activity sheet, noting interesting responses. After you notice that groups have discussed #2, pause and bring the class together for a whole-class discussion. </item>
  <item>Call on groups to share out some of their names. Write them on the board. If there are a lot of responses, circle a few that you will agree mean input, output, and function or operation.Allow students the freedom to use the names they prefer.</item>
  


<image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6f0608c5e977814d21d6e7162e63590c42fdf766" height="300"/><item>After you notice that individuals have finished #3, pause and bring the class together for a whole-class discussion.</item> 
<item>Call on students to share out their definitions in #3. Lead class in combining the various answers to write a definition as a class. </item>
<item>When you notice groups discussing #4, give a 2 minutes time warning.</item>

</list>
<title>Student Activity</title>

<div class="os-raise-familysupport">
  <para><link url="https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/function-builder/latest/function-builder_en.html">Access the interactive simulator</link> to begin this project.</para>
</div>


<para>In this activity, whenever you see 💬, stop and share your responses with your partner. If you have different responses, try to come to a consensus.
</para>

<para>In the first activity, you may have noticed that all of the simulation screens share a three-part structure. These three parts can be described in a variety of ways, such as the ones listed in the image below. </para>

<image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6f0608c5e977814d21d6e7162e63590c42fdf766" height="300"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated"><para/><item>Come up with your own names or labels for the three parts of a function.</item>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis><list>
      <item>Front loader, inserts, domain, original, start</item>
      <item>processor, machine, coder, producer, action</item>
      <item>product, yield, after, end</item>
    </list>


<para/><item> Share your labels with your group. Write down any the similarities. 💬</item>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
<para>For questions 3-4, use the following instructions.</para>
<blockquote>Use the simulation screen labeled “Patterns”. Note you can switch between simulation screens using the toolbar located below the function builder.</blockquote>
<item>The blue “builder” in the middle of the screen is a function. Describe what a function is using the labels you've agreed on as a class. 💬</item>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>


